#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "e052cb8a-7337-57ef-99b4-65a1c742c4bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-620" :question/problem "The velocity vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle u, v \\rangle$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n$\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 12t+t^2, t^3+1\\rangle$.\nCalculate the acceleration vector $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{du}{dt}, \\frac{dv}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(12t + t^{2}), \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3} + 1)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨12 + 2t, 3t^{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "88214928-ff75-51ac-bb97-f04b1f45360b" :question/math-academy-id "e-621" :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 4t^2+1, 3t^2-t\\rangle$.\nCalculate the acceleration vector $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector. We can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{dx}{dt}, \\frac{dy}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(4t^{2} + 1), \\frac{d}{dt}(3t^{2} - t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨8t, 6t - 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{du}{dt}, \\frac{dv}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(8t), \\frac{d}{dt}(6t - 1)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨8, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087559 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "c01d6202-76ac-5a5c-850f-5b699d01c462" :question/math-academy-id "e-7224" :question/problem "The velocity vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle u, v \\rangle$, measured in $\\textrm{m}/\\textrm s$, of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds, is given by\n$\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t^4-t^2, 2t^3\\rangle$.\nFind the acceleration vector and the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{du}{dt}, \\frac{dv}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t^{4} - t^{2}), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨4t^{3} - 2t, 6t^{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1$, we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(1) &= ⟨4(1)^{3} - 2(1), 6(1)^{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $\\mid \\mathbf a(1) \\mid$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a(1) & &= & ⟨2, 6⟩\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2^{2} + 6^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{40} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{10}m/s^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087560 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "b7bffc21-d43c-5b2b-b09b-649d17b1eb96" :question/math-academy-id "e-7225" :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$, in meters, of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n$\\mathbf{r} = \\left\\langle t^{5}-t^2,\\, t^2+6t-3 \\right\\rangle$.\nCalculate the acceleration vector and the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=0$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\frac{dr}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{dx}{dt}, \\frac{dy}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t^{5} - t^{2}), \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} + 6t - 3)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5t^{4} - 2t, 2t + 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{du}{dt}, \\frac{dv}{dt}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(5t^{4} - 2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t + 6)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨20t^{3} - 2, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=0$, we substitute $t=0$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(0) &= ⟨20(0)^{3} - 2, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 2, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $\\mid \\mathbf a(0) \\mid$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a(0) & &= & ⟨ - 2, 2⟩\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{8} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}m/s^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94c87ef4-516b-5fd3-946b-8f9c62c98016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = ⟨5, 2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086300} {:answer/id #uuid "9e58aea6-19dc-5488-bc80-93d7632e221f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = ⟨ - 8, 4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086301} {:answer/id #uuid "39afe4ac-830b-58c4-b06b-d3fb4983f0bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = ⟨5t, 2t^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086302} {:answer/id #uuid "5f00365e-8d58-5b9f-b448-aa58586fbb31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = ⟨5, 4t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086303} {:answer/id #uuid "6586d8bf-7a12-561b-af59-2bdb282646dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = ⟨5, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f00365e-8d58-5b9f-b448-aa58586fbb31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = ⟨5, 4t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086303} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8eeedcb-7380-5490-8e80-bea8245eee54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086299}] :question/id #uuid "fca59699-e72b-5149-821a-1daf54baaef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-7156" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v = ⟨u, v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$v = ⟨5t - 8, 2t^{2} + 4⟩$.\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$."}]}
